res, one in each row and one in each column.  \nDenote by  \n\n\\[\na_i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\nthe column of the uncovered square in row \\(i\\).  \nBecause each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the map  \n\n\\[\ni\\mapsto a_i\n\\]\n\nis a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nFor each column \\(j\\) let  \n\n\\[\nb_j=\\{i\\mid a_i=j\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the (unique) row containing the uncovered square of column \\(j\\); thus \\(b_{a_i}=i\\).\n\nA *tile* is an axis‑aligned rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; it covers all unit squares inside it.  \nTiles are required to be pairwise disjoint and must avoid the uncovered squares.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\)\n\nFor every row \\(i\\) with \\(a_i>1\\) define the *left‑adjacent* cell  \n\n\\[\nL_i=(i,a_i-1).\n\\]\n\nExactly one row has \\(a_i=1\\); consequently the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{L_i\\mid a_i>1\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains precisely \\(n-1\\) cells.  \nSimilarly, for every row \\(i\\) with \\(a_i<n\\) define the *right‑adjacent* cell  \n\n\\[\nR_i=(i,a_i+1),\n\\]\n\nand let  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R=\\{R_i\\mid a_i<n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich also has cardinality \\(n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 1.** *No rectangle that avoids all uncovered squares can contain two distinct cells of \\(\\mathcal L\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose a rectangle \\(T\\) contains \\(L_i\\) and \\(L_k\\) with \\(i<k\\).  \nWrite the row interval of \\(T\\) as \\([r_1,r_2]\\) and its column interval as \\([c_1,c_2]\\).  \nSince \\(L_i=(i,a_i-1)\\in T\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nr_1\\le i\\le r_2,\\qquad c_1\\le a_i-1\\le c_2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,a_i)\\) and \\(T\\) must avoid it, the column interval cannot reach column \\(a_i\\); hence  \n\n\\[\nc_2\\le a_i-1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAnalogously, from \\(L_k=(k,a_k-1)\\in T\\) we o