n  \n\n\\[\nc_2\\le a_k-1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(c_2\\le\\min\\{a_i,a_k\\}-1\\).  \nBut (1) tells us that \\(c_2\\ge a_i-1\\) and \\(c_2\\ge a_k-1\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\na_i-1\\le c_2\\le a_i-1\\quad\\text{and}\\quad a_k-1\\le c_2\\le a_k-1,\n\\]\n\nwhich forces \\(a_i=a_k\\), contradicting the fact that the \\(a_i\\)’s are distinct. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 2.** *No rectangle can contain simultaneously a cell of \\(\\mathcal L\\) and a cell of \\(\\mathcal R\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a rectangle \\(T\\) contains \\(L_i=(i,a_i-1)\\) and \\(R_j=(j,a_j+1)\\).  \nIts column interval \\([c_1,c_2]\\) must contain both \\(a_i-1\\) and \\(a_j+1\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nc_1\\le a_i-1\\le a_j+1\\le c_2 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the interval contains the column \\(a_i\\) (since \\(a_i-1<a_i\\le a_j+1\\le c_2\\)).  \nBut \\((i,a_i)\\) is an uncovered square, so \\(T\\) would cover a forbidden cell – a contradiction. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nFrom Lemma 1 each rectangle can cover **at most one** element of \\(\\mathcal L\\); therefore at least \\(|\\mathcal L|=n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover all cells of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nSimilarly Lemma 1 applied to the set \\(\\mathcal R\\) gives another lower bound of \\(n-1\\) rectangles for \\(\\mathcal R\\).  \nLemma 2 shows that a rectangle covering a cell of \\(\\mathcal L\\) cannot at the same time cover a cell of \\(\\mathcal R\\).  \nHence any tiling must use at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  An explicit tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) do the following:\n\n* If \\(a_i>1\\), place a horizontal tile covering the cells  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le a_i-1\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. the interval from the left edge of the board up to the column immediately left of the uncovered square.\n\n* If \