\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nMatilda chooses a set of \\(n\\) uncovered squares, one in each row and one in each column.  \nWrite  \n\n\\[\na_i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\nfor the column of the uncovered square in row \\(i\\).  \nBecause each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the map  \n\n\\[\ni\\longmapsto a_i\n\\]\n\nis a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\nA *tile* is an axis\u2011aligned rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; it covers all unit squares inside it.  \nTiles must be pairwise disjoint and must avoid the uncovered squares.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\)\n\nFor every row \\(i\\) with \\(a_i>1\\) define the *left\u2011adjacent* cell  \n\n\\[\nL_i=(i,a_i-1).\n\\]\n\nExactly one row has \\(a_i=1\\); hence the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{L_i\\mid a_i>1\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains precisely \\(n-1\\) cells.  \nSimilarly, for every row \\(i\\) with \\(a_i<n\\) define the *right\u2011adjacent* cell  \n\n\\[\nR_i=(i,a_i+1),\n\\]\n\nand let  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R=\\{R_i\\mid a_i<n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich also has cardinality \\(n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 1.** *A rectangle that avoids all uncovered squares can contain at most one element of \\(\\mathcal L\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a rectangle \\(T\\) contains two distinct cells \\(L_i\\) and \\(L_k\\) with \\(i\\neq k\\).  \nWrite the column interval of \\(T\\) as \\([c_1,c_2]\\) (inclusive).  \nSince \\(L_i=(i,a_i-1)\\in T\\), we have \\(c_1\\le a_i-1\\le c_2\\).  \nBecause the uncovered square \\((i,a_i)\\) must be avoided, the rectangle cannot reach column \\(a_i\\); therefore \\(c_2\\le a_i-1\\).  \nThus \\(c_2=a_i-1\\).  \nThe same reasoning applied to \\(L_k\\) yields \\(c_2=a_k-1\\).  \nHence \\(a_i-1=a_k-1\\), i.e. \\(a_i=a_k\\), contradicting the fact that the \\(a_i\\)\u2019s are all distinct (they form a permutation). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 2.** *A rectangle that avoids all uncovered squares cannot contain simultaneously a cell of \\(\\mathcal L\\) and a cell of \\(\\mathcal R\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose a rectangle \\(T\\) contains \\(L_i=(i,a_i-1)\\) and \\(R_j=(j,a_j+1)\\).  \nLet its column interval be \\([c_1,c_2]\\).  \nThen \\(c_1\\le a_i-1\\) and \\(c_2\\ge a_j+1\\).  \nConsequently the interval \\([c_1,c_2]\\) contains every integer column between \\(a_i-1\\) and \\(a_j+1\\), in particular the columns \\(a_i\\) and \\(a_j\\).  \nThus \\(T\\) would cover the uncovered squares \\((i,a_i)\\) or \\((j,a_j)\\), which is forbidden. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 each rectangle can cover **at most one** element of \\(\\mathcal L\\); therefore at least \\(|\\mathcal L|=n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover all cells of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nThe same argument applied to \\(\\mathcal R\\) gives another lower bound of \\(n-1\\) rectangles for \\(\\mathcal R\\).  \nLemma\u202f2 shows that a rectangle covering a cell of \\(\\mathcal L\\) cannot at the same time cover a cell of \\(\\mathcal R\\).  \nHence any tiling must use at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) proceed as follows.\n\n*If \\(a_i>1\\):* place a horizontal tile covering the cells  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le a_i-1\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. the interval from the left edge of the board up to the column immediately left of the uncovered square.\n\n*If \\(a_i<n\\):* place a horizontal tile covering the cells  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\mid a_i+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. the interval from the column immediately right of the uncovered square to the right edge of the board.\n\nEach such tile is a rectangle of size \\(1\\times (a_i-1)\\) or \\(1\\times (n-a_i)\\); it never contains the uncovered square of its row.  \nTiles belonging to different rows lie in distinct rows, so they are pairwise disjoint.  \n\nExactly one row has \\(a_i=1\\) (hence no left\u2011hand tile) and exactly one row has \\(a_i=n\\) (hence no right\u2011hand tile).  \nTherefore the total number of tiles placed is  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\{i\\mid a_i>1\\}\\bigr|+\\bigl|\\{i\\mid a_i<n\\}\\bigr|\n=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles always exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion\n\nThe lower bound and the explicit construction coincide, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
