                "content": "### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have found a complete solution.  \nThe minimum possible number of rectangular tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n1. **Observation on holes.**  \n   The uncovered unit squares must form a permutation matrix: exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column.  \n\n2. **Row‑segment argument.**  \n   In any row whose uncovered square is not at the extreme left (column 1) or extreme right (column \\(n\\)), the covered squares of that row are split into two non‑adjacent blocks (left of the hole and right of the hole).  \n   A single axis‑parallel rectangle cannot contain both blocks, because a rectangle is a contiguous product of a set of rows and a set of columns. Hence each such row needs **at least two** tiles to cover its squares.  \n\n3. **Edge‑hole optimisation.**  \n   A row whose uncovered square lies in column 1 (or column \\(n\\)) has its covered squares in a single contiguous block, so it can be covered with **one** tile.  \n   Because each column may contain only one uncovered square, at most one row can have its hole in column 1 and at most one row can have its hole in column \\(n\\). Consequently, at most two rows can be covered with a single tile.  \n\n4. **Counting a lower bound.**  \n   Let \\(k\\) be the number of rows whose hole is at an edge (so \\(k\\le 2\\)).  \n   The remaining \\(n-k\\) rows each require at least two tiles.  \n   Hence any tiling uses at least  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\,(n-k)+k = 2n-2\n   \\]\n\n   tiles.  \n\n5. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose a permutation in which one row has its hole in column 1, another (different) row has its hole in column \\(n\\), and all other rows have their holes elsewhere (any bijection for the remaining columns works).  \n\n   * For the two edge rows we place a single horizontal tile covering the whole row except the hole.  \n   * For each of the remaining \\(n-2\\) rows we place two horizontal tiles: one covering the cells left of the hole, the other covering the cells right of the hole.  \n\n   All tiles are axis‑parallel, their interiors are disjoint, and every row and column contains exactly one uncovered square.  \n   The total number of tiles is  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\,(n-2)+2 = 2n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit construction both give the same value, so the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet the board be the set  \n\n\\[\nB=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n\\},\\qquad n=2025 .\n\\]\n\nA placement of tiles is a partition of a subset \\(C\\subseteq B\\) into axis‑parallel rectangles, where  \n\n* each rectangle consists of all unit squares whose row indices belong to a consecutive interval\n  \\([r_1,r_2]\\) and whose column indices belong to a consecutive interval \\([c_1,c_2]\\);\n* the rectangles are pairwise disjoint;\n* the uncovered squares form a set \\(U=B\\setminus C\\) such that each row and each column\n  contains exactly one element of \\(U\\).\n\nThus \\(U\\) is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a bijection \\(\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound\n\nFix a row \\(i\\).  \nIf \\(\\pi(i)=1\\) (hole at the left edge) then the covered squares of row \\(i\\) are the\nconsecutive block \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid 2\\le j\\le n\\}\\); a single rectangle can cover them.\nSimilarly, if \\(\\pi(i)=n\\) the row can be covered by one rectangle.\nIf \\(1<\\pi(i)<n\\) the covered squares of row \\(i\\) consist of two disjoint blocks  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<\\pi(i)\\},\\qquad\nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid \\pi(i)<j\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich are separated by the uncovered square \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).\nA rectangle is a product of a set of rows and a set of columns; therefore a rectangle\ncontaining a cell of \\(L_i\\) cannot also contain a cell of \\(R_i\\) (otherwise it would also contain the hole \\((i,\\pi(i))\\)).  \nConsequently **any tiling must use at least two distinct rectangles to cover the squares of such a row**.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nk=\\bigl|\\{i\\mid \\pi(i)=1\\text{ or }\\pi(i)=n\\}\\bigr|\n\\]\n\nbe the number of rows whose hole lies at an edge.  \nBecause each column can contain at most one hole, at most one row can have \\(\\pi(i)=1\\) and at most one row can have \\(\\pi(i)=n\\); hence \\(k\\le 2\\).\n\nAll other \\(n-k\\) rows each need at least two rectangles.  \nThus any admissible tiling uses at least  \n\n\\[\n2\\,(n-k)+k = 2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose a permutation \\(\\pi\\) as follows:\n\n* pick a row \\(r_1\\) and set \\(\\pi(r_1)=1\\);\n* pick a different row \\(r_2\\) and set \\(\\pi(r_2)=n\\);\n* on the remaining \\(n-2\\) rows choose any bijection onto the remaining\n  columns \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\nNow place tiles:\n\n* **Edge rows.**  \n  For \\(r_1\\) place the horizontal rectangle covering the cells\n  \\(\\{(r_1,j)\\mid 2\\le j\\le n\\}\\); for \\(r_2\\) place the rectangle\n  \\(\\{(r_2,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-1\\}\\).  \n  Each of these rows is covered by a single tile.\n\n* **Non‑edge rows.**  \n  For every other row \\(i\\) (so \\(1<\\pi(i)<n\\)) place two horizontal rectangles:\n  \\[\n  L_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<\\pi(i)\\},\\qquad\n  R_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid \\pi(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n  \\]\n  These two rectangles are disjoint, together cover all squares of row \\(i\\), and avoid the hole \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).\n\nAll tiles are axis‑parallel, their interiors are pairwise disjoint, and every row and column contains exactly one uncovered square (by construction of \\(\\pi\\)).  \nThe total number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n2\\,(n-2