ong to a\n  consecutive interval \\([c_1,c_2]\\);\n* the interiors of the rectangles are pairwise disjoint;\n* every square of \\(B\\) belongs either to exactly one rectangle or to none.\n\nThe condition \u201ceach row and each column has exactly one uncovered square\u2019\u2019\nmeans that the set of uncovered squares  \n\n\\[\nU = B\\setminus\\bigcup_{R\\in\\mathcal{T}}R\n\\]\n\ncontains precisely one element in each row and each column.  \nHence there exists a bijection \\(\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus the uncovered squares form a *permutation matrix*.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Geometry of a single rectangle\n\nLet \\(R\\in\\mathcal{T}\\) be a rectangle covering rows\n\\(I=[r_1,r_2]\\) and columns \\(J=[c_1,c_2]\\).\nIf for some \\(i\\in I\\) we have \\(\\pi(i)\\in J\\), then \\((i,\\pi(i))\\in R\\),\ncontradicting (1). Consequently, for every row \\(i\\in I\\) the hole\n\\(\\pi(i)\\) lies **outside** the column interval \\(J\\).\n\nHence a rectangle is of one of the two exclusive types:\n\n* **Left rectangle:** for all \\(i\\in I\\) we have \\(\\pi(i)>c_2\\); i.e. the whole\n  rectangle lies strictly to the **right** of the holes of its rows.\n* **Right rectangle:** for all \\(i\\in I\\) we have \\(\\pi(i)<c_1\\); i.e. the whole\n  rectangle lies strictly to the **left** of the holes of its rows.\n\nNo rectangle can contain cells on both sides of the hole of any of its rows.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound for left rectangles\n\nConsider the rows in the natural order \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\) and define the\n*running minimum*\n\n\\[\nm_i=\\min\\{\\pi(j)\\mid j\\ge i\\},\\qquad 1\\le i\\le n .\n\\]\n\nThe sequence \\((m_i)\\) is non\u2011increasing; it can drop only when we encounter a\nrow whose hole column is smaller than all holes below it.\n\nSuppose a left rectangle \\(R\\) covers a set of rows \\(I=[a,b]\\).\nBecause \\(R\\) is left, its rightmost column \\(c_2\\) must satisfy\n\\(c_2<\\pi(i)\\) for every \\(i\\in I\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nc_2 < \\min_{i\\in I}\\pi(i)=\\min\\{\\pi(i)\\mid a\\le i\\le b\\}=m_a .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently, all rows whose left cells are covered by the *same* left\nrectangle must have the **same** value of the running minimum \\(m_i\\); otherwise\n(2) would be violated.\n\nWhenever the running minimum strictly decreases, i.e. \\(m_i<m_{i+1}\\), the\nleft cells of row \\(i\\) cannot be covered by the same left rectangle that\ncovers the left cells of any later row. Hence each strict drop forces a new\nleft rectangle.\n\nThe running minimum can drop at most \\(n-1\\) times (from at most \\(n\\) down to\n\\(1\\)). The only rows that do **not** create a drop are those with\n\\(\\pi(i)=1\\), because then the left side of the row is empty and no left\nrectangle is needed for that row.\n\nThus the number \\(L\\) of left rectangles satisfies  \n\n\\[\nL\\ge (n-1)-\\mathbf{1}_{\\{\\exists i:\\pi(i)=1\\}} .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIn particular, \\(L\\ge n-1\\) unless there is a row whose hole lies in column\u202f1,\nin which case that row needs no left rectangle.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound for right rectangles\n\nAn entirely symmetric argument, scanning the rows from bottom to top, uses the\n*running maximum*\n\n\\[\nM_i=\\max\\{\\pi(j)\\mid j\\le i\\},\\qquad 1\\le i\\le n .\n\\]\n\nA right rectangle covering rows \\(I=[a,b]\\) must satisfy\n\\(c_1>\\max_{i\\in I}\\pi(i)=M_b\\); therefore each strict increase of the\nrunning maximum forces a new right rectangle. The running maximum can increase\nat most \\(n-1\\) times, and a row with \\(\\pi(i)=n\\) has an empty right side.\nHence the number \\(R\\) of right rectangles obeys  \n\n\\[\nR\\ge (n-1)-\\mathbf{1}_{\\{\\exists i:\\pi(i)=n\\}} .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Combining the bounds\n\nBecause the holes form a permutation, at most one row can satisfy \\(\\pi(i)=1\\)\nand at most one (different) row can satisfy \\(\\pi(i)=n\\).  \nConsequently the two indicator terms in (3) and (4) together contribute at most\n\\(2\\) to the right\u2011hand sides.\n\nAdding (3) and (4) we obtain a universal lower bound for the total number\n\\(|\\mathcal{T}|\\) of rectangles:\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{T}| = L+R \\;\\ge\\; (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  A construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose a permutation \\(\\pi\\) with exactly one row \\(r_1\\) such that\n\\(\\pi(r_1)=1\\) and one different row \\(r_2\\) such that \\(\\pi(r_2)=n\\); on the\nremaining \\(n-2\\) rows pick any bijection onto the set\n\\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\n* **Edge rows.**  \n  For row \\(r_1\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n  \\(\\{(r_1,j)\\mid 2\\le j\\le n\\}\\).  \n  For row \\(r_2\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n  \\(\\{(r_2,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-1\\}\\).\n\n* **All other rows.**  \n  For each row \\(i\\) with \\(1<\\pi(i)<n\\) place two horizontal rectangles:\n  \\[\n  L_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<\\pi(i)\\},\\qquad\n  R_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid \\pi(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n  \\]\n\nAll these rectangles are axis\u2011parallel, pairwise disjoint, and together cover\nevery square of the board except the holes \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).  \nThe number of rectangles used is  \n\n\\[\n2\\;(n-2)\\;+\\;2 \\;=\\; 2n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Minimality\n\nInequality (5) shows that no placement can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) rectangles,\nwhile the construction above achieves exactly \\(2n-2\\). Therefore the bound is\ntight, and the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2n-2}= \\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\nThus Matilda can meet the requirement with 4048 rectangular tiles, and it is\nimpossible to do so with fewer."
