l tile covering the cells  \n\n   \\[\n   (i,1),(i,2),\\dots ,(i,i-1)\n   \\]\n\n   (the left part of the row).  \n   For each row \\(i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\)) place a horizontal tile covering  \n\n   \\[\n   (i,i+1),(i,i+2),\\dots ,(i,n)\n   \\]\n\n   (the right part of the row).  \n\n   These tiles are pairwise disjoint, each tile is a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines, and every unit square except the diagonal ones is covered exactly once.  \n   The total number of tiles is  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) is achievable, therefore it is optimal.  \n   For \\(n=2025\\) we obtain the minimal number  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet the board consist of the unit squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\) where \\(n=2025\\).\nDenote by \\(\\sigma(i)\\) the column of the unique uncovered square in row \\(i\\); the condition on the columns forces \\(\\sigma\\) to be a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\sigma(i)-1,\\qquad R_i=n-\\sigma(i).\n\\]\n\nThus the uncovered square splits row \\(i\\) into a left block of \\(L_i\\) squares and a right block of \\(R_i\\) squares.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Lower bound\n\nConsider the left blocks.  \nIf \\(L_i>0\\) then the square \\((i,1)\\) must be covered by some tile \\(T\\).  \nSince a tile is a rectangle, all squares of \\(T\\) lie in a set of consecutive columns.\nBecause \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) is uncovered, column \\(\\sigma(i)\\) cannot belong to \\(T\\); consequently every column of \\(T\\) is \\(\\le L_i\\).\n\nHence \\(T\\) can be used only for rows whose uncovered column lies **to the right** of all columns of \\(T\\); i.e. for rows \\(k\\) with \\(\\sigma(k) > L_i\\).  \nIf two rows \\(i\\) and \\(j\\) have different values of \\(L\\), say \\(L_i<L_j\\), then a tile covering \\((i,1)\\)